 "interpretation": "The standard real-vs-random-permutation signal is real but trivial: the deterministic local type-compatibility rule reaches 0.9989, and the binding head with the compatibility bit matches it.  The hard control preserves that compatibility and the end-type marginal, so the oracle rule and the marginal head are exactly 0.5; the remaining hard signal is a simple pair/context frequency statistic.  A no-training context-conditioned table using root/mid degree, ring flags, min-ring, closure, atom type, and bond type reaches 0.6514 on hard, above the binding head's 0.5919.  The binding head is not reliably above the simple pair-frequency table or the pair-only linear head, and it transfers worse than the linear model to unseen local combinations.  Under the requested strict criterion this is not a nontrivial binding signal; do not start a GPU pretraining experiment on this basis.",
